<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah — Betzi'as HaPas<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">(Part 1: §§ 10–21)</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span> — continuation of Halacha 6.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 10 — The Laws of <i>Betzi'as HaPas</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">betzi'as hapas</span></span> [breaking the bread], in which there are many laws concerning the breaking. Our Sages investigated and expounded and debated at length regarding the breaking, as is written in the Gemara <span class="sr">(Sanhedrin 102)</span>: <span class="v">"From where do you begin <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>?"</span> etc. — from which it is understood that there are deep secrets in this (as was already explained in the preceding <span class="h">halachos</span>).</div>

<div class="p">For the entire <span class="h">tikkun</span> of <span class="h">betzi'as hapas</span> that our Sages were so precise about — is that one needs to break the bread with the <span class="h">berachah</span> in such a manner that he merits at the time of eating the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> explained in the Torah teaching. For it is explained there that through the inclusiveness of the son and student, one merits at the time of eating the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. For the <span class="h">Malchus</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span> — receives sustenance from the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, which are the <span class="h">remazim</span>; there the <span class="h">makifin</span> [surrounding lights] shine, which are the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>, the aspect of silence. And this is the aspect of <span class="mx">"one does not converse during a meal"</span> — see there well.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 11 — The Ten Fingers on the Bread</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what is written in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>: that one must <span class="kc">place his ten fingers upon the bread</span> at the time of breaking — to indicate that the bread one eats is drawn from the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> mentioned above. Through this, the holiness of eating — whose essence is to merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> drawn through the hands, which are the <span class="h">remazim</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 12 — Break from Where the Fire Struck Most</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"From where do you begin <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>? From the place that crusted (<span class="h">d'karim b'shulah</span>)."</span> For the eating of Israel must be in great holiness. And the primary food that satisfies a person is the <span class="kc">bread</span>. Therefore it has <span class="kc">ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span></span>, as written in the Tur and <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>, for one must refine the grain and the bread through many stages of refinement. This is the aspect of all the labors and exertions invested in grain until it becomes bread: plowing, sowing, reaping, separating from chaff and straw, then grinding, sifting, and cleaning from bran and husks, etc.</div>

<div class="p">All of this derives from the sin of Adam HaRishon, whose sin was in eating — he ate from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, and then it was decreed upon him: <span class="v">"By the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread"</span> <span class="sr">(Genesis 3:19)</span> — which is all the labors mentioned above until it becomes bread. And all of this is for the sake of <span class="kc">refining</span> — to eliminate the bad and the waste and to refine the good.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, the essential spiritual refinement is accomplished by Israel through the ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span> pertaining to bread. And all these refinements — refining the good from the bad, the food from the waste, cleaning the grain from chaff and straw (which are the <span class="h">klipos</span> that precede the fruit) — are all to refine the <span class="kc">holy <span class="h">emunah</span></span> that Adam HaRishon blemished. This is accomplished by driving away and eliminating from the mind the <span class="h">kushyos</span> [difficulties/questions] in every person's heart, from which all forms of heresy derive — which are the <span class="h">klipos</span>, the chaff and straw covering the wheat. As is understood from the <span class="h">Tikunai Zohar</span> and the Holy Zohar regarding the refinement of the <span class="h">halachah</span> — which is the aspect of refining the wheat from the chaff and straw — for it is all one matter.</div>

<div class="p">For through eating, vitality is drawn to a person. And the essential vitality is <span class="h">da'as</span> and <span class="h">chochmah</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"Wisdom gives life"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 7:12)</span>. Therefore food at its root — especially bread, which is the main thing — is very exalted, for rooted within it is power and vitality that is the aspect of <span class="h">chochmah</span> to give life to a person and bind his soul to his body. But since the sin of Adam HaRishon, much waste surrounds the bread — chaff and straw, etc. — which are the <span class="h">kushyos</span>, the confusion of <span class="h">da'as</span> that surround the mind until one cannot understand the <span class="h">halachah</span> properly. These are the laws of the holy Torah, and for this one must labor greatly to refine the <span class="h">halachah</span> from the <span class="h">kushyos</span> — the chaff and straw — as is found in the <span class="h">Tikunai Zohar</span>. And even more, one must refine the bread from the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and waste that seek to confuse holy <span class="h">emunah</span> — for this is the main thing.</div>

<div class="p">For the primary rectification of eating is through <span class="h">emunah</span>, as explained elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I, 62</span>). For the primary sustaining and completeness of <span class="h">da'as</span> is through <span class="h">emunah</span>, as <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> explains many times. And this itself is the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, the aspect of silence mentioned above, that one merits during eating — each according to what he merits to receive from his true <span class="h">Rebbe</span>, the illumination of son and student. For the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> comes essentially through the completeness of <span class="h">emunah</span>, merited when the <span class="h">Rebbe</span> illuminates with his wondrous <span class="h">da'as</span> that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> etc., as understood from the Torah teaching and the holy <span class="h">sichah</span> he spoke with us afterward, brought in <span class="h">Sipurai Ma'asiyos</span> — see there.</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, when one comes to eat the bread — which is made from grain already refined through many stages from chaff and straw, bran and husks (which are the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and confusions), all of which derives from the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> of the <span class="h">Rav</span> who is the aspect of Moshe, illuminating the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> in each person to know that <span class="v">"Hashem — He is the G-d"</span> — but the ultimate completeness of <span class="h">da'as</span> is <span class="kc">silence</span>, the aspect of <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span>, the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And therefore, when one wants to eat the bread — for then the primary drawing of vitality, the <span class="h">chochmah</span> and <span class="h">da'as</span>, must be drawn through this bread — therefore the entire <span class="h">tikkun</span> is to eat it with <span class="h">berachah</span>, in holiness and purity, so that through eating this bread one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, which is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> and completeness of the <span class="h">da'as</span> that has been refined up to this point through all the labors performed on the grain until it became bread.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore our Sages cautioned to be very precise at the time of breaking — how to break it — so that one breaks and divides the bread and eats it in holiness, meriting to receive the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during the eating. And this is: <span class="v">"From where do you begin <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>? From where it crusted (<span class="h">d'karim b'shulah</span>)"</span> — the place where the <span class="kc">fire struck most</span>. For fire is the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span>, as explained elsewhere. For the primary <span class="h">tikkun</span> of all the above is through <span class="h">yirah</span>, as explained in the Torah teaching and in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"A strap would extend…"</span> (<span class="lr">LM II, 77</span>) — that the primary <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating is through <span class="h">yirah</span>. Through this, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn during eating. For <span class="h">yirah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">tzimtzum</span> [contraction] — the aspect of silence. When one reveals <span class="h">da'as</span> to his companions and students and nullifies many <span class="h">kushyos</span> and heresies from their minds — yet the mind still wants to run and fly to understand more and more — then one must make a fence for his words, which is silence, and hint to him: <span class="kc">"Thus far you may come"</span>, for it is forbidden for you to investigate further according to your level, for <span class="v">"into that which is too wondrous for you, do not investigate"</span> — as explained in the Torah teaching regarding the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span>: that each time a <span class="h">makif</span> is brought in, another <span class="h">makif</span> immediately enters, etc. — see there well.</div>

<div class="p">And this fence and <span class="h">tzimtzum</span> — which is silence — comes through <span class="h">yirah</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">tzimtzum</span>. For the main thing is to draw upon him <span class="h">yirah</span> until he has fear and dread and shame to investigate with his coarse <span class="h">da'as</span> into that which he has no permission. For even the great <span class="h">tzadik</span> himself — the aspect of Moshe — contracts his mind out of <span class="h">yirah</span> and does not gaze at comprehensions of G-dliness above the level where he currently stands. This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"And Moshe hid his face, for he feared to gaze upon G-d"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 3:6)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must break the <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> from <span class="h">d'karim b'shulah</span> — the place where the <span class="kc">fire struck most</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span>, <span class="h">tzimtzum</span>. For the entire completion of the bread's <span class="h">tikkun</span> to be fit for human eating is through the fire — the <span class="h">chochmah</span> and <span class="h">da'as</span> in the bread that gives life are completed through <span class="h">tzimtzum</span>, which is silence, which is <span class="h">yirah</span>, which is fire. Therefore one breaks the bread from the place the fire struck most — in order to merit during this eating the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, drawn through silence, drawn through <span class="h">tzimtzum</span>, drawn through fire and <span class="h">yirah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 13 — Breaking, Chewing, and Refining the <i>Halachah</i></div>

<div class="p">For initially, when the bread is whole, it is impossible to eat it as is — until one breaks and divides and shatters it into pieces and then chews it. Only then does it give life to a person. And all of this is the <span class="kc">refinement of the <span class="h">halachah</span></span> — the refinement of <span class="h">da'as</span>. For after one eliminates all the waste — the straw and chaff from the <span class="h">halachah</span> — one still needs to <span class="kc">chew the <span class="h">halachah</span></span>, to understand it thoroughly and properly, to internalize it in the heart. For <span class="h">teretz</span> [answer] has the connotation of breaking — meaning the <span class="h">terutzim</span> to the <span class="h">kushyos</span> are the aspect of breaking, shattering and nullifying the <span class="h">kushyos</span> until one knows the <span class="h">halachah</span> properly and divides and decides the <span class="h">halachah</span> into its many parts and legal rulings. Then — the essential completeness of the refinement of the <span class="h">halachah</span>. And the same applies to the inner <span class="h">da'as</span> — to comprehend and know Hashem.</div>

<div class="p">All of this is the aspect of breaking the bread and shattering it to be fit for eating. Therefore one must be very precise about the breaking — that <span class="kc">the <span class="h">berachah</span> should conclude together with the bread</span> — that the breaking should be only in the aspect of the <span class="h">berachah</span>, which is the holy <span class="h">emunah</span> in its simplicity: that we believe Hashem created the bread, and therefore we bless Him. Meaning: not to enter further into <span class="h">kushyos</span> and investigations at all — for this was the entire sin of Adam HaRishon in eating from the Tree of Knowledge: <span class="mx">"he gazed at what he had no permission to gaze at."</span> Now we must rectify this through Israel's eating with a <span class="h">berachah</span>. Therefore one breaks from the place where the fire struck most — the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span> — for the primary <span class="h">tzimtzum</span> of <span class="h">da'as</span> is through <span class="h">yirah</span>, through which the fence and partition of silence is drawn, through which one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating — through the hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> from which all sustenance [derives]. For it is all interdependent, as explained in the Torah teaching.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — Blessing on the Whole Loaf</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must be precise to recite the <span class="h">berachah</span> on the <span class="kc">whole</span> [loaf], which has greater importance. For the essential completeness is through <span class="h">yirah</span>, as <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> brings from the Holy Zohar: <span class="mx">"In the place where <span class="h">yirah</span> is found, there you find completeness"</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"for there is no lack to those who fear Him"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 34:10)</span>. For one must eat in holiness in a manner that draws <span class="kc">satisfaction</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"the righteous eats to satisfy his soul"</span> <span class="sr">(Proverbs 13:25)</span>, unlike the wicked of whom it is said: <span class="v">"the belly of the wicked shall lack."</span> The primary satisfaction is through the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"You open Your hand and satisfy every living thing with <span class="h">Ratzon</span>"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 145:16)</span>. And the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn through <span class="h">yirah</span>. Therefore one must bless on the whole — which is the aspect of <span class="h">yirah</span>, where the essential importance resides, as explained in the Torah teaching on the <span class="h">chatzotzros</span> (<span class="lr">LM I, 5</span>): that the essential importance is <span class="h">yirah</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"the fear of Hashem is His treasure."</span></div>

<!-- PESACH SECTION -->
<div class="pesach-head">Heads of Chapters for the Laws of Pesach<br>Connected to Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400;">Based on the Torah teaching "Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — Pesach: The <i>Tzadik</i> Who Illuminates Upper and Lower</div>

<div class="p">And all of this is the aspect of <span class="kc">Pesach</span>. (See Hilchos Peter Chamor, Halacha 4, where several matters regarding <span class="h">matzah</span> and the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span> are explained according to the Torah teaching.) The main point: the primary completeness of man, for which he was created, is that he should <span class="kc">join the upper dwellers with the lower dwellers</span> — as explained above. This is accomplished in completeness only through the <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation, exceedingly great in stature, who knows how to illuminate in the upper and lower worlds in the aspect of <span class="v">"for all in heaven and earth"</span> <span class="sr">(I Chronicles 29:11)</span> — to join and include them together, as explained in the Torah teaching (<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>) — see there well.</div>

<div class="p">And each person, according to his closeness to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, receives this illumination upon himself — through which his <span class="h">tikkun</span> is eternal. For through this illumination, <span class="kc">he will never fall and never become distant</span>, even if something passes over him. For as long as he draws close to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> holds his hand and informs him each time that Hashem is still with him and near him, that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — and awakens him each time not to despair in any manner, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"Awaken and sing, dwellers of the dust"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 26:19)</span> — as explained there.</div>

<div class="p">And all of this is the aspect of Pesach — the first redemption, through Moshe Rabbainu, who is the primary aspect of the true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> who can illuminate in the upper and lower worlds and join them together. And so too now, in this bitter exile — all our hope and all our redemption and the release of our souls that we hope for and strive to draw upon ourselves each day, in the aspect of <span class="v">"draw near to my soul, redeem it"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 69:19)</span> — is all through the power of these true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are the aspect of Moshe. <span class="kc">He was the first redeemer and he will be the final redeemer</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"that which was, is that which will be"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 1:9)</span>. And he is with us even now in our exile, for <span class="mx">"his extension is in every generation"</span>, and he illuminates within us through true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> that Hashem is still with us and with each and every individual, whatever his situation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 16 — Pesach, <i>Matzah</i>, and <i>Maror</i></div>

<div class="p">And upon this hint the <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span>, <span class="h">matzah</span>, and <span class="h">maror</span>. For the <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span> is the aspect of <span class="kc">inclusiveness of the worlds</span> — the upper in the lower, the lower in the upper — which is the essential completeness of the redemption, as explained in <span class="h">Sipurai Ma'asiyos</span> on the verse <span class="v">"You redeemed the tribe of Your inheritance, Mount…"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 74:2)</span>, which relates to the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam."</span> For it is explained there that this is the aspect of the sacrifices: the animal ascended as a pleasing fragrance to Hashem above, and Hashem, as it were, descended below into the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And in the <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span>, this illumination shone with even greater wonder — for even in the land of Egypt, which was filled with idols, Moshe Rabbainu shone with his essential power so that they could offer the <span class="h">Korban Pesach</span> even there, to raise the animal to Hashem from such an impure place, and to bring Hashem Himself down there, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"And I shall pass through the land of Egypt… I am Hashem — I and not an agent… I am He and no other"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 12:12)</span>. This is the essential aspect of the upper below and the lower above — the inclusiveness of the worlds. And through this, the essential redemption.</div>

<div class="p">But it is already explained that all of this is impossible to grasp — only the <span class="h">tzadik</span> illuminates in us from afar that Hashem is still with us. But it is <span class="kc">forbidden to enter into the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span></span>, for <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — especially each person at the beginning of his drawing close. And as long as it is for him the aspect of a beginning — for after he went back and ruined, as he knows in his soul — certainly he needs now to return and begin anew, as explained in our words at length. Therefore, according to his lowly level, it is certainly impossible for him to know all this. Rather, they illuminate in him in a manner that he can <span class="kc">strengthen himself in <span class="h">emunah</span></span> that Hashem is still with him — but G-d forbid that he enter into <span class="h">kushyos</span> and <span class="h">terutzim</span> that are, for his level, above time, where time does not suffice to explain them, as explained in the Torah teaching.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">matzah</span></span> — that we are very careful then about <span class="h">chametz</span>: not to begin to "leaven" one's mind at all with investigations and <span class="h">kushyos</span>, as explained in Hilchos Peter Chamor — see there well.</div>

<div class="p">And this is <span class="kc"><span class="h">maror</span></span> — in memory of <span class="v">"they embittered their lives."</span> For through the <span class="h">Pesach</span> and <span class="h">matzah</span>, great and mighty illuminations of mind are drawn upon every member of Israel, until each strengthens himself in his place to know that Hashem is still with him, that <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — the inclusiveness of the worlds — the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>, until one merits the silence mentioned above, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating. But all this is impossible to merit except through the aspect of <span class="h">maror</span> — for one must <span class="kc">endure bitterness</span> before meriting this.</div>

<div class="p">As explained (<span class="lr">LM I, 27</span>) on the verse <span class="v">"Behold, for <span class="h">shalom</span> — it was bitter to me, bitter"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 38:17)</span> — <span class="h">shalom</span> is healing for everything, healing of the soul and healing of the body. But it is impossible to receive <span class="h">shalom</span> except through bitterness. Only that Hashem has mercy and does not send bitterness according to one's many sins — which he could not endure — but only according to his ability. And this is the eating of <span class="h">maror</span> on the night of Pesach: the inclusiveness of the worlds that one merits on Pesach is the aspect of <span class="h">shalom</span> — peace between soul and body, between right and left, between upper and lower. And this <span class="h">shalom</span> cannot be merited except through bitterness. Therefore one eats <span class="h">maror</span> together with the <span class="h">Pesach</span> and <span class="h">matzah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 17 — Bitterness in Many Forms</div>

<div class="p">The bitterness one must endure takes many forms. One must endure literal bitterness from the many obstacles and entanglements in drawing close to the point of truth, and obstacles from livelihood, and sometimes bodily suffering, or from children, G-d forbid. And also the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and confusions that warp the heart, from which one must flee — primarily through the aspect of silence. This too is bitterness, in the aspect of <span class="v">"they embittered their lives with hard labor"</span> — <span class="h">b'kushya</span> [with difficulty/questions] — as explained elsewhere. And in many ways, this bitterness is the hardest of all, for the main thing is to <span class="kc">break the obstacles of the mind</span> that come from the <span class="h">kushyos</span> and the crookedness in the heart.</div>

<div class="p">But whoever wants to have pity on himself and escape from the pit of destruction and the miry clay — he must endure these bitternesses according to his level, and then he will merit <span class="h">shalom</span>: the bond and inclusiveness of the worlds. But one must know and believe that Hashem sends the bitterness with compassion, casting all his sins behind His back, sending him bitterness not according to his sins but only according to his ability.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc">dipping the <span class="h">maror</span> in <span class="h">charoses</span></span>. Our Sages said: <span class="mx">"Hashem had compassion upon us"</span> — meaning: according to his deeds, he would not have the strength to endure the bitterness due to him, but Hashem diminishes the bitterness. <span class="h">Chas</span> [compassion] = <span class="h">Charoses</span>. <span class="h">Rus</span> [Ruth] alludes to the aspect of David who <span class="mx">"saturated Hashem with songs and praises"</span>, as our Sages say <span class="sr">(Bava Basra 14b)</span>. Meaning: the sweetening of the bitterness comes through saturating [Hashem] with songs and praises — knowing the truth that everything is through kindness and mercy, and Hashem has compassion upon us and diminishes the bitterness. Therefore we must thank Him and praise Him with songs and praises, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"In Hashem I will praise His word, in G-d I will praise [His word]"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 56:11)</span> — for everything is through great kindness.</div>

<div class="p">Also, the <span class="h">charoses</span> alludes to the clay, as our Sages said, for they embittered their lives with mortar and brick. Meaning: one must remember that according to his deeds he was sunk in the miry clay, and Hashem raised him from there in the aspect of: <span class="v">"He lifted me out of the pit of roaring, from the miry clay"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 40:3)</span>. Therefore certainly one must endure this bitterness, for everything is through kindness and mercy according to his deeds.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 18 — <i>Marah</i>: The Test After the Sea</div>

<div class="p">And this is why immediately after the splitting of the sea it is written: <span class="v">"And they came to Marah, and they could not drink the waters of Marah… there He established for him statute and judgment, and there He tested him"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 15:23-25)</span>. For this is the essential test for every person who begins to draw close: he must endure the bitterness that passes over him.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"And He said: if you will listen diligently to the voice of Hashem your G-d… all the disease I placed upon Egypt I will not place upon you, for I am Hashem your healer"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 15:26)</span>. Our Sages were troubled by this verse, but according to the above it is well explained: He promises them a great promise — that even though according to their former deeds it would be fitting for all the diseases of Egypt to come upon them, G-d forbid (for it is impossible to be healed except through bitterness), yet Hashem promises that in His kindness He will have pity and not place upon them all the disease of Egypt, for then they would not be able to endure and would despair. Rather, He will lighten [the load] in every way possible and send only according to their ability. This is: <span class="v">"For I am Hashem your healer"</span> — meaning: I, Hashem, who am a great Compassionate One, am engaged in your healing. Therefore I will certainly not send bitterness according to your deeds, but will heal you with mercy — with a little bitterness according to your ability, so that you will endure and be healed.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 19 — The Four Cups of Wine and <i>Kiddush</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc">four cups of wine</span>. For wine in holiness is the aspect of the <span class="kc">revelation of <span class="h">Ratzon</span></span>, in the aspect of <span class="mx">"whoever is enticed by his wine…"</span> And: <span class="v">"Your palate is like the finest wine, going to my Beloved with rectitude"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 7:10)</span> — Rashi explains: love of rectitude — the aspect of love and yearning and wondrous <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. And this is visible to the senses: one who is G-d-fearing and drinks wine in holiness on Shabbos and <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, his heart is aroused with intense yearning for Hashem. For when one merits, wine gladdens him, the aspect of <span class="v">"wine gladdens the heart of man"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 104:15)</span> — and joy is the expansion of the heart in intense yearning for Hashem, the aspect of <span class="v">"in Your presence is the fullness of joys"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 16:11)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc">cup of wine for <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span></span>: to reveal the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> that is drawn during eating through the inclusiveness of son and student.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="kc"><span class="h">Kiddush</span> over wine on Shabbos and <span class="h">Yom Tov</span></span>. For <span class="mx">"song is only said over wine"</span>, and song is the essential <span class="h">d'veikus</span> to Hashem with wondrous <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. Therefore, upon the entrance of Shabbos — when one must elevate all of the creation of the six weekdays and rectify all the weekday activities to be included in the holiness of Shabbos (for this is their essential purpose: when they ascend and are included in Shabbos, which is the purpose of heaven and earth) — for Shabbos is the aspect of the World to Come, and the essential purpose of this world created in six days is to be included in the World to Come, which is Shabbos — the inclusiveness of the worlds. Therefore immediately upon the entrance of Shabbos, one must make <span class="h">Kiddush</span> over wine, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — for the essential inclusiveness is through <span class="h">Ratzon</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For the main thing is to draw compassion upon the world, to illuminate in them the <span class="h">da'as</span>, to remove them from the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> [spirit of folly] from which all sins and blemishes derive. Whose primary hold is during the six weekdays, in the aspect of <span class="v">"the people went out and gathered"</span> — <span class="h">b'shtusa</span> [in folly]. But on Shabbos, the <span class="h">da'as</span> shines — the <span class="h">da'as</span> of the <span class="h">Rav</span> illuminating in son and student. For the <span class="h">tzadik</span> is the aspect of Shabbos. And therefore one makes <span class="h">Kiddush</span> over wine — for the completeness of the inclusiveness is the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. And <span class="mx">"there is no <span class="h">Kiddush</span> except in the place of a meal"</span> — to draw the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating, for then the essential illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, from which the primary satisfaction [derives] — <span class="v">"and [You] satisfy every living thing with <span class="h">Ratzon</span>."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 20 — The Four Cups Correspond to the Four Sons</div>

<div class="p">But on <span class="kc">Pesach</span> — the beginning of the redemption that occurred through a wondrous miracle and great kindness, before the giving of the Torah, when they were redeemed without <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> [arousal from below] — therefore one needs to draw the <span class="h">da'as</span> mentioned above through <span class="kc">many changes</span> [in the Seder format], as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>. This is the aspect of the <span class="kc">four cups</span> corresponding to the four expressions of redemption: <span class="v">"I will take out," "I will save," "I will redeem,"</span> and <span class="v">"I will take."</span></div>

<div class="p">For on Shabbos, one elevates the six weekdays through the <span class="h">Kiddush</span>, through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — through the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> performed during the week. But on Pesach, which was before the giving of the Torah when there was no <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — rather, great illuminations of mind are drawn from above at the entrance of Pesach through His kindness alone, beginning with <span class="h">Kiddush</span> over wine — therefore one cannot eat the meal immediately. For now it is impossible to draw and illuminate the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> in completeness except by drinking a second cup before the meal and reciting the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> over it: telling the miracles and wonders Hashem did for us, that He took us out of Egypt in His kindness — <span class="v">"in the beginning, our fathers served idols, and now He has brought us close"</span> — through which the essential <span class="kc">inclusiveness of the lower worlds in the upper worlds</span> [is accomplished]: the inclusiveness of <span class="h">Asiyah</span> [Action] in <span class="h">Atzilus</span> [Emanation].</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="kc">wise son illuminating the wicked son</span>, who is in the aspect of <span class="h">Asiyah</span>. It is impossible to refine <span class="h">Asiyah</span> and illuminate <span class="h">da'as</span> there except through <span class="h">Atzilus</span>, as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>. For the main thing is to rectify the wicked son — the aspect of <span class="h">Asiyah</span> — drawing down the very exalted <span class="h">da'as</span> with wondrous wisdom until it illuminates in <span class="h">Asiyah</span>, to bring the wicked to <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. For the lower and more corrupted [a person is], the higher the source of intellect and <span class="h">da'as</span> that must be drawn down to him (as explained in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Mishrah"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">For the four cups correspond to the four sons, as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>. Therefore, through the second cup upon which the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> is recited — which corresponds to <span class="h">Asiyah</span>, to be included in <span class="h">Atzilus</span> — through this the essential inclusiveness of the worlds, the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>, through which one merits the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. Therefore one cannot eat beforehand, for now it is impossible to merit the inclusiveness except through reciting the <span class="h">Haggadah</span> over the second cup. And therefore the plate is removed beforehand — to indicate that now it is impossible to eat in the aspect of the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> except through telling of the Exodus.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 21 — The Child Asks and the Answer Is: <i>Emunah</i></div>

<div class="p">And all of this is so that a child sees and asks <span class="v">"Mah nishtanah"</span> — for here, the son asks. It is necessary that the son — they are the four sons — should ask. For the entire purpose of the Exodus, where He revealed His G-dliness before all eyes, was for the sake of <span class="kc">the children</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"And you shall make known to your children and your children's children"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 4:9)</span>, and in many verses. For this is the essential <span class="h">da'as</span>: to illuminate in the children and students (who are also called children), to illuminate the <span class="h">da'as</span> from generation to generation forever, the aspect of <span class="v">"generation to generation shall praise Your works"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 145:4)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one makes changes [at the Seder] so that [the child] will ask. But the entire answer is only: <span class="v">"We were slaves to Pharaoh in Egypt, and Hashem our G-d took us out from there"</span> — and one elaborates in this telling. Yet in all of this, there is still no fully satisfying answer and definitive reason for all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> and the changes — as is obvious to everyone and as explained in the <span class="h">seforim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">But in truth, this is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>: to inform [the child] that all the changes of this night, and all the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, are all to draw upon us the holy <span class="h">emunah</span> that He revealed to us in His mercy through the Exodus — through the awesome signs and wonders He performed for us beyond measure. Through which we saw with our own eyes that it is fitting for us to serve Him and accept His kingship with love, as He commanded us through Moshe. <span class="kc">But the essential reason of every <span class="h">mitzvah</span> in detail — this is impossible to explain</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"I have seen an end to every purpose; Your commandment is exceedingly broad"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 119:96)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is what is written regarding the wise son's answer: <span class="v">"One does not eat after the <span class="h">Pesach</span> any <span class="h">afikoman</span>"</span> (see Hilchos Peter Chamor). And to all [four sons], the answer given is: <span class="v">"With a strong hand Hashem took us out of Egypt"</span> — to say: for this reason we eat <span class="h">matzah</span>, <span class="h">maror</span>, etc.; and for this reason we make changes so that you will ask, and they will tell you the miracles and wonders of the Exodus, and from this you will understand it is fitting for us to fulfill the words of Moshe who transmitted all this to us. But the essential reason in detail — certainly it is impossible to tell you.</div>

<div class="p">And the <span class="kc">wicked son</span>, who asks with audacity: <span class="v">"What is this service to you?"</span> — excluding himself from the collective and denying the foundation — therefore, we blunt his teeth and tell him: <span class="v">"For me and not for him; had he been there, he would not have been redeemed."</span> Since you exclude yourself from the collective and ask <span class="v">"What is this service?"</span> — as if to say he does not want to fulfill this service unless they explain to him explicitly the reason of every matter, which is impossible. Therefore we distance him and tell him: had he been there, he would not have been redeemed. For <span class="kc">the redemption was only through <span class="h">emunah</span></span> — <span class="v">"and the people believed"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 4:31)</span>. And as it is written: <span class="v">"Come, gaze from the summit of <span class="h">amanah</span> [faith]"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 4:8)</span>. Only that it was impossible for us to come to <span class="h">emunah</span>, and therefore He performed for us such miracles and wonders, and showed us that it is fitting to believe. And Israel, the holy nation, did so and looked at the truth and <span class="v">"believed in Hashem and in Moshe His servant."</span></div>

<div class="p">But to explain to you the <span class="h">da'as</span> in detail — that you should know the reason of every service — this is impossible. And all our intention even in the answer to the wicked son is for his benefit — perhaps he will return from his wickedness and humble his heart not to ask such wicked <span class="h">kushyos</span>. And as the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> have already accomplished: many wicked people returned in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> through their words, which instilled in them [the desire] to look at the truth and not ask excessive <span class="h">kushyos</span>, since the <span class="h">da'as</span> has already shone upon them that it is fitting for them to believe, through all the signs and wonders.</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§10–14 (Betzi'as HaPas)</strong> develop the mystical architecture of bread-breaking: the ten fingers on the bread = the ten <em>remazim</em>-fingers in <em>yam hachochmah</em>. Breaking from where the fire struck most = <em>yirah</em> = <em>tzimtzum</em> = silence = the illumination of <em>Ratzon</em>. The process of grain into bread parallels refining <em>halachah</em> from <em>kushyos</em>, and refining <em>emunah</em> from heresy. Chewing the bread = "chewing" the <em>halachah</em> to internalize it. The <em>berachah</em> concluding with the breaking = <em>emunah</em> in simplicity, rectifying Adam's sin of gazing where he had no permission. The whole loaf = completeness through <em>yirah</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§15–21 (Pesach)</strong> frame the entire Seder through LM II:7. Pesach = the first redemption through Moshe, the true <em>Rebbe</em> who joins upper and lower. As long as one draws close to the <em>tzadik</em>, he will never fall — the <em>tzadik</em> holds his hand and informs him Hashem is still with him. <em>Matzah</em> = not "leavening" the mind with <em>kushyos</em>. <em>Maror</em> = the bitterness one must endure (obstacles, livelihood, mental questions) before meriting <em>shalom</em>. Dipping in <em>charoses</em> = Hashem's compassion diminishing the bitterness. <em>Marah</em> after the sea = the test every newcomer faces. The four cups = revelation of <em>Ratzon</em> through wine; <em>Kiddush</em> = including this-worldly weekdays in the World to Come. On Pesach, without <em>is'arusa d'l'sata</em>, the <em>Haggadah</em> over the second cup includes <em>Asiyah</em> in <em>Atzilus</em>. The four sons = four levels to illuminate. The answer to all four: <em>emunah</em> — the detailed reasons are impossible to explain; redemption came only through believing.</p>
